ty \\(\\phi_\\pi(G)\\), of a numbering \\(\\pi\\) of \\(G\\), is the quantity of valid 2-paths of \\(G\\) induced by \\(\\pi\\). The *minimum* (*maximum*) validity \\(\\phi_{\\min}(G)\\) (\\(\\phi_{\\max}\\)) of \\(G\\) is the minimum (maximum) of \\(\\phi_\\pi(G)\\) over all possible numberings of \\(G\\). \\(\\phi_{\\min}(G)\\) (\\(\\phi_{\\max}\\)) can also be stated as the decision problem MIN-VP(G, k) (MAX-VP(G, k)), which asks “Is there a numbering \\(\\pi\\) of \\(G\\) such that \\(\\phi_\\pi(G) \\le k\\) (\\(\\phi_\\pi(G) \\ge k\\))?”\n\nThis work presents the main results recently achieved in [3]. Those results include a proof that MAX-VP(G, k) is an \\(NP\\)-complete problem (even in regular graphs of degree 3 (cubic graphs)), and the behaviour of the \\(\\phi_{\\min}(G)\\) and \\(\\phi_{\\max}(G)\\) functions in some other graph classes.\n\n## References\n\n[1] L. Foulds and H. J. Longo. Valid path-based graph vertex numbering, 2023. arXiv eprint 2306.02200.\n\n[2] J. A. Gallian. A dynamic survey of graph labeling. *The Electronic Journal of Combinatorics*, Dec. 21 2022. Twenty-first edition, #DS6.\n\n[3] G. Leal. Valid path numberings in some graph classes. Master’s thesis, Universidade Federal de Goiás, 2024. Under development.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe average distance in a random graph (https://fanchung.ucsd.edu/wp/aveflong.pdf)\nciteturn10search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Fan Chung † ... Of particular interest are power law random graphs in which the number of vertices of degree ... the average distance of the power law graphs is almost surely of order log n/ log ˜d.\nThe average distance in a random graph\nwith given expected degrees∗\n(Full version)\nFan Chung †\n‡\nLinyuan Lu†\nAbstract\nRandom graph theory is used to examine the “small-world phenomenon” – any two strangers\nare connected through a short chain of mutual acquaintances. We will show that for certain\nfamilies of random graphs with given expected degrees, the average distance is almost surely of\norder log n/ log ˜d where ˜d is the weighted average of the sum of squares of the expected degrees.\nOf particular interest are power law random graphs in which the number of vertices of degree\nk is proportional to 1/kβ for some ﬁxed exponent β. For the case of β > 3, we prove that\nthe average distance of the power law graphs is almost surely of order log n/ log ˜d. However,\nmany Internet, social and citation networks are power law graphs with exponents in the range\n2 < β < 3 for which the power law random graphs have average distance almost surely of\norder log log n, but have diameter of order log n (provided having some mild constraints for the\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nErdős distinct distances problem (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erd%C5%91s_distinct_distances_problem)\nciteturn10search16 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; *  Chung, Fan (1984). \"The number of different distances determined by ${\\displaystyle n}$ points in the plane\" (PDF). ... MR 1134448.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGraffiti (program) (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graffiti_%28program%29)\nciteturn10search17 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; One of Graffiti's most cited results is the residue-independence number conjecture, which states that for any graph G, the residue R(G) is at most the independence number α(G). ... Other notable results include bounds on average distance proved by Fan Chung in 1988. ... *  DeLaViña, Ermelinda (January 2003), Some History of the Development of Graffiti (PDF), University of Houston-Downtown.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nГипотеза Эрдёша о числе различных расстояний (https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%93%D0%B8%D0%BF%D0%BE%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%B7%D0%B0_%D0%AD%D1%80%D0%B4%D1%91%D1%88%D0%B0_%D0%BE_%D